reasoning:
Borrow: `let V::Func(wf) = &w` borrows w; then `let mut a = wf.attrs.borrow_mut();` holds; at end we return w while `a` is still alive. Need to drop `a` before returning. Also `x.attrs.borrow()` iteration nested. Restructure: do the mutation in a block, drop borrow, then return w.

```
fn update_wrapper(w: V, f: &V) -> V {
    let wf = match &w {
        V::Func(wf) => wf.clone(),
        _ => return w,
    };
    let V::Func(x) = f else {
        return w;
    };
    wf.qualname.set(x.qualname.get());
    let mut a = wf.attrs.borrow_mut();
    for (k, v) in x.attrs.borrow().iter() { ... }
    ...
    drop(a);
    w
}
```

wf is Rc<Func> — clone borrows nothing. Func's attrs is the same cell via Rc. Then drop(a) before returning w. Also the `x.attrs.borrow()` temporary inside the for loop — it's dropped at end of each iteration? `for (k, v) in x.attrs.borrow().iter()` — the Ref temp lives for the whole loop. It borrows x's cell, separate from wf's cell. But if w and f are the same function (wraps applied to itself?), wf.attrs and x.attrs are the same cell → borrow_mut held + borrow → panic. Edge case: `@functools.wraps(f)` where wrapper is f itself? CPython update_wrapper(w, w) — would copy dict into itself; our impl would panic on double borrow. Could guard: if Rc::ptr_eq(wf, x) skip the attrs loop. Let me add that to be safe:

```
if !Rc::ptr_eq(wf, x) {
    let mut a = wf.attrs.borrow_mut();
    for (k, v) in x.attrs.borrow().iter() { ... }
    if !a.iter().any(...) { a.push(...) }
}
```

But even for distinct functions this is fine. Note Func fields: attrs: RefCell<Vec<...>>. wf is Rc<Func> clone. Let me rewrite.

